course: dictionary
lesson: 2753
title: Disappointing and letting down
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Disappointing means not as good as you hoped or expected. It makes you feel let down. «The film was disappointing, and the ending was flat.» You expected a good film, but it was not good, so you feel let down.
explain: Look at the words around disappointing to check its meaning. «The result was disappointing after weeks of training.» Weeks of training means the team hoped to win, so a disappointing result means they did not win or did not do as well as they hoped.
explain: Disappointing can also be a verb form, from disappoint. In «The news disappointing» the word means letting someone down. Try your meaning in the sentence: if «not as good as hoped» still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: Disappointing means not as good as you hoped or expected.
fact: Disappointing makes you feel let down.
fact: Common phrases: «a disappointing result», «it is disappointing that», «rather disappointing».
fact: «A damp squib» (UK) means a disappointing failure, like a firework that does not go off.
fact: As a verb, disappointing means letting someone down, from the word disappoint (disappoint, disappoints, disappointed, disappointing).
exercise: «The film was disappointing, and the ending was flat.» What does disappointing mean? | Long and full of songs | Very funny and exciting | *Not as good as you hoped | Very loud and noisy
exercise: Dad says, «The result is disappointing.» What does he mean? | The result was made of paper | The result came very early in the day | *The result is not as good as they hoped | The result is wonderful and everyone cheered
exercise: «A damp squib» means a firework that does not go off. What does the phrase mean? | A loud and brilliant bang | A cheerful song | *A disappointing failure | A cold winter's night
test: «It was a disappointing meal, cold and tasteless.» Which phrase means not as good as hoped? | *a disappointing meal | a loud cheer | a bright sunny day
test: «The team lost again, and the fans were ___.» Which word fits best? | ancient | *disappointed | sleepy | delighted
exam: «The rain ruined our picnic, which was rather ___.» Which word fits best? | famous | luminous | thrilling | *disappointing
